# Nonvanishing of dx for a separating transcendental element

Theorem statement: `KaehlerDifferential.D_ne_zero_of_transcendental`
(`0df85775-aaa5-5911-8954-96e03b12839a`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `95ed7aea-228a-552c-bed7-c51720bd6f7e`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field, $F$ a field, and let $F$ be a $K$-algebra. Let $x \in F$ be transcendental over
$K$, i.e. `Transcendental K x`: $x$ is not a root of any nonzero polynomial over $K$. Assume further
that $F$ is a separable algebra over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K
{x}` generated by $x$, that is, every element of $F$ is algebraic with separable minimal polynomial
over $K(x)$. The conclusion is that the universal derivation applied to $x$, namely
`KaehlerDifferential.D K F x` $= dx$, is nonzero in the module of Kähler differentials
$\Omega_{F/K}$ of $F$ over $K$. This is the nonvanishing half only: the statement asserts $dx \neq
0$, not that $dx$ is an $F$-basis of $\Omega_{F/K}$, although the proof establishes the stronger
fact along the way.

This is the classical nonvanishing of the differential of a separating element of a function field,
underlying the fact that $\Omega_{F/K}$ is one-dimensional over $F$ with basis $dx$. It is used
throughout the treatment of differentials on curves, for instance in defining and computing the
order of a differential at a place and in the study of torsion in the degree-zero part of the Picard
group.

